# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): selected and described from the Rijksmuseum's linked open data,
# its backs in six languages written and checked against the sources, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://id.rijksmuseum.nl/260213> ,
              <https://iiif.micr.io/> ,
              <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zelfportret_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-3262.jpeg> ,
              <https://www.wikipedia.org/> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "Selection: the Rijksmuseum’s own “Top 100” set (https://id.rijksmuseum.nl/260213), 133 object records, read through the museum’s Search API and Linked Art resolver on 2026-10-10. Left out: the five that are already in the library’s Famous paintings deck (The Night Watch SK-C-5, The Milkmaid SK-A-2344, The Jewish Bride SK-C-216, The Little Street SK-A-2860, Woman Reading a Letter SK-C-251, matched by their Wikidata items’ inventory numbers) and the four whose pictures are in copyright (Jan Schoonhoven’s Dish Relief SK-A-5007, Carel Visser’s Eight Stacked Beams BK-2008-4, Karel Appel’s Square Man SK-A-5002, Gerrit Rietveld’s armchair for Til Brugman BK-2010-1). Records that are parts of one work became one card, under the record of the whole or of its first part: the ten weepers of Isabella of Bourbon (BK-AM-33-A to J, under BK-AM-33), the two flower pyramids and the tiers of the second (BK-2004-4-B and its parts, under BK-2004-4-A), five Javanese court officials (NG-2010-37 to 41), the wine fountain and its cooler (BK-1963-64, -65), two toilet caskets (BK-2009-255-1, -2), two temple guardians (AK-RAK-2007-1-A, -B), the two halves of Piero di Cosimo’s portraits of the Sangallos (SK-C-1367, -1368) and Jamnitzer’s table ornament and its plaque (BK-17040-A, -A-1). That leaves 93 cards. Each card’s id is the object number of the record it uses, in lower case."@en ,
                 "Front: the object’s picture, from the Rijksmuseum’s IIIF image service (one, Van Gogh’s Self-portrait, from Wikimedia Commons). There is no picture description: any description of these objects would either name them or say nothing a sighted learner does not see."@en ,
                 "Back: “title — maker, date”, as in the Famous paintings deck. The English title is the museum’s brief English title, or the name it is known by where the brief title gives one (“… Known as ‘The Merry Drinker’”). The maker is the museum’s maker statement, with “signed by artist” and “mentioned on object” dropped and “attributed to” and “workshop of” kept; for an anonymous work the place where the record says it was made stands in its place. Dates follow the record, written in each language’s way (c., ca, um, vers, 년경). In Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French, the title is the object’s Wikidata label where it is a genuine title in that language, else the title that language’s Wikipedia gives it, else a translation of the museum’s English title checked against its Dutch one; makers and places are their Wikidata labels in that language, or the form that language’s Wikipedia uses where the label is an unusual variant; Korean names Wikidata does not give are transliterated by the Korean rules for Dutch (외래어 표기법). Three cards have a back note: the clock and gunpowder horn left on Novaya Zemlya, the ten weepers, and the series of five Javanese court officials."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the deck’s content comes from the Rijksmuseum’s records (CC0 1.0 metadata, pictures marked public domain), Wikidata (CC0 1.0) and one public-domain picture on Wikimedia Commons, so the deck is CC0 1.0 like the rest of the library. Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0) gave only the titles of works and forms of names, which are facts, and was otherwise used to verify. As the Rijksmuseum asks, though it does not require it, the deck credits it as the source of its data and pictures."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and pictures: every record’s rights, every picture URL, overlap with the library"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the 133 records of the Top 100 set, the 93 cards made from them, their pictures and the deck metadata."@en ,
                 "Outcome: every one of the 124 records the cards use states CC0 1.0 for its data and the Public Domain Mark 1.0 for its picture (checked by a script over the records’ JSON-LD); the four In Copyright records and the five already in Famous paintings were left out; each of the 93 picture URLs was fetched and returned a JPEG (HTTP 200)."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Backs in six languages (AI): written, then checked by a second AI agent"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: every card’s back, in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French, in six batches: each batch written by an AI agent from the Rijksmuseum’s records, Wikidata and Wikipedia by the rules in the compilation’s comments, then checked by a second AI agent told to find what is wrong (a title that is not the established one in its language, a name in an unusual form, a date or qualifier that is wrong, a merged card that does not name the whole, unnatural Korean) and to correct it."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 31 corrections, among them the common forms of makers’ names where a Wikidata label was an unusual variant (Jan Willem Pieneman, Jacob van Ruisdael), dates given exactly by the museum that had been written “circa”, Korean names transliterated by the Dutch rules (판피아넌, 혼더쿠터르, 피스허르, 흐레호르, 세자르), genuine German titles in place of coined ones (Die Goldene Bucht in der Herengracht; Klagende vom Prunkgrab der Isabella von Bourbon), and one card the first agent had left out (the silver cabinet, BK-R-4927). The compiler then made the German Rembrandt “Rembrandt van Rijn” on every card, as in Famous paintings."@en .

<https://id.rijksmuseum.nl/260213>
    dcterms:title "Rijksmuseum: Top 100 (a set of its collection’s linked open data)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Rijksmuseum" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Content source: the selection (the set’s 133 object records), each card’s object, title, maker or place of making and date, from the records’ Linked Art JSON-LD at https://id.rijksmuseum.nl/ (Search API https://data.rijksmuseum.nl/search/collection?memberOfSetId=https://id.rijksmuseum.nl/260213, read 2026-10-10). Every one of the 124 records the cards use states its own rights: its data record “subject_to” CC0 1.0, its picture (VisualItem) the Public Domain Mark 1.0. The Rijksmuseum’s data policy (https://data.rijksmuseum.nl/policy, read 2026-10-10): “For much of the information and data on the collection website, you will find a Public Domain or Creative Commons Zero (CC0) Public Domain Dedication under the copyright notice, meaning you can use the material freely”; “In some cases, copyright does apply. If so, a Creative Commons BY 4.0 (CC BY 4.0) licence is indicated”; “We kindly ask that when using our information and data, you credit the Rijksmuseum … even if this is not required.” The four records of the set whose pictures are “In Copyright” (rightsstatements.org InC 1.0) were left out."@en .

<https://iiif.micr.io/>
    dcterms:title "Rijksmuseum pictures (IIIF Image API, served by Micrio)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Rijksmuseum" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Content source: the picture on each card but one, hotlinked from the IIIF image service that the Rijksmuseum’s records name as their pictures’ access point (DigitalObject access_point, e.g. https://iiif.micr.io/PJEZO/full/max/0/default.jpg), at most 1280 pixels on a side. Each picture’s VisualItem is classified with the Public Domain Mark 1.0 (“Public Domain”) in the record, read 2026-10-10."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Content source: the names of makers and places in Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French (their labels, reached through the Wikidata links in the Rijksmuseum’s records), and objects’ titles in those languages where an object has a Wikidata item (found by its inventory number, P217 qualified by collection P195 Rijksmuseum, Q190804) with a genuine title as its label. Wikidata’s structured data is CC0 1.0 (https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing)."@en .

<https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zelfportret_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-3262.jpeg>
    dcterms:title "Zelfportret Rijksmuseum SK-A-3262.jpeg (Wikimedia Commons)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikimedia Commons contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Content source: the picture of Vincent van Gogh’s Self-portrait (SK-A-3262), whose Rijksmuseum record states a public-domain picture but names no access point; File:Zelfportret Rijksmuseum SK-A-3262.jpeg, marked “Public domain” on Commons with the Rijksmuseum’s collection page as its credit, read 2026-10-10."@en .

<https://www.wikipedia.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikipedia (English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikipedia contributors" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "Verification, and the established title of a work or form of a name in a language where Wikidata had none or a wrong one: an article’s title only (a name, not a creative expression), never text from an article. Wikipedia’s text is CC BY-SA 4.0 (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Copyrights)."@en .

